WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.196
Factory Engineering, demo three. One real feature, from a GitHub
issue to a draft pull request.

2
00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:15.812
The banner on each shot says what it is: live, a saved real run,
or an owner decision.

3
00:00:16.112 --> 00:00:25.112
In this recording, an AI agent, called Agent, clicked every
owner decision at the owner's request. So the review is an agent
review.

4
00:00:28.912 --> 00:00:34.912
Needs you is the inbox. It lists one next action for each task,
with blocked work first.

5
00:00:36.912 --> 00:00:43.320
These are real open items of the project, for example old
experiment runs that still wait for a review.

6
00:00:46.912 --> 00:00:52.480
The Board shows every task in its stage, from intake to done,
with its warnings.

7
00:01:02.912 --> 00:01:10.256
The new issue, number fourteen, asks for a Complete button on
each open task, and a new API endpoint.

8
00:01:11.912 --> 00:01:17.264
Factory lists the issues with the label factory, but it imports
one only on request.

9
00:01:19.912 --> 00:01:22.960
The import creates a work item. Nothing runs yet.

10
00:01:30.912 --> 00:01:36.576
Triage turns the issue into a work request. A model reads the
issue and the code.

11
00:01:37.912 --> 00:01:44.728
Every paid call starts with a card: the exact conversation, and
its money limit, here twenty cents.

12
00:01:46.912 --> 00:01:48.592
Agent confirms the card.

13
00:01:51.912 --> 00:01:57.408
The agent now reads files. Each file it reads becomes evidence
for its design choice.

14
00:02:12.912 --> 00:02:19.056
The draft has six requirements. Four are checked by tests, and
two need a human review of the page.

15
00:02:23.912 --> 00:02:32.672
The design assessment explains the choice: extend the feature
folder, and add a small in-memory store, because the seed data
must not change.

16
00:02:38.912 --> 00:02:45.728
It lists the eighteen files that triage read. If one of them
changes before the run, Factory blocks the run.

17
00:02:54.912 --> 00:02:59.760
Triage suggests a design review, because the change adds a new
interaction.

18
00:03:01.912 --> 00:03:07.216
A new UI pattern would need a committed HTML mockup, merged
before its approval.

19
00:03:08.912 --> 00:03:16.232
Here the button follows the existing list pattern. So Agent
waives the review, with a reason, on the owner's behalf.

20
00:03:26.912 --> 00:03:33.032
The request uses the repository's allowed paths. A request can
narrow them, but never widen them.

21
00:03:34.912 --> 00:03:40.624
Agent approves the request. Its digest is now fixed, and Factory
posts it on the issue.

22
00:03:48.912 --> 00:03:57.144
Before any code is written, a separate QA agent writes
acceptance tests from the approved request. The coding agent
never sees them.

23
00:04:00.912 --> 00:04:05.208
Again a card, with a limit of fifty cents. Agent confirms it.

24
00:04:09.912 --> 00:04:13.752
Factory then runs the new tests on the unchanged base commit.

25
00:04:20.912 --> 00:04:25.712
Eight tests. On the unchanged base, one passes and seven fail.

26
00:04:26.912 --> 00:04:32.744
For a new feature, that is expected: the failing tests need the
new endpoint or the new button.

27
00:04:36.912 --> 00:04:41.664
The tests build, so the coding agent can use the interface note
that comes with them.

28
00:04:52.845 --> 00:04:58.893
Agent approves the test set. It becomes a protected check of the
run, bound to its digest.

29
00:05:03.845 --> 00:05:07.205
Keep this decision in mind. It will matter in a moment.

30
00:05:20.612 --> 00:05:25.028
First, a real safety stop from an earlier task, issue number
eight.

31
00:05:27.612 --> 00:05:35.220
The agent needed files outside the allowed paths. Verification
rejected them, and the agent stopped with a scope conflict.

32
00:05:39.612 --> 00:05:44.892
Since then, triage and approval check the paths before any money
is spent on a run.

33
00:05:56.612 --> 00:06:03.980
The run card shows the exact attempt, the base commit, the
model, the money limit, and the protected QA tests.

34
00:06:07.612 --> 00:06:09.484
Agent confirms it.

35
00:06:12.612 --> 00:06:18.060
Live run activity shows each step of the agent while it works.
The waiting time is cut.

36
00:06:40.612 --> 00:06:47.380
The first run passed the build, all unit tests, the acceptance
tests, the UI check, and the scope check.

37
00:06:50.612 --> 00:06:53.660
But two of the eight protected QA tests failed.

38
00:07:06.112 --> 00:07:15.448
Here is what happened. Before the code existed, the QA agent
wrote eight tests. On the old code, seven failed, as expected
for a new feature.

39
00:07:15.748 --> 00:07:24.628
In run one, the two failing tests were wrong, not the code. One
expects two open tasks where one is correct. One cannot start
its browser.

40
00:07:24.928 --> 00:07:31.456
On the old code, a wrong test fails just like a correct one. So
the check before the run could not see it.

41
00:07:33.256 --> 00:07:37.312
This is the approved QA test set, on the work item page.

42
00:07:43.756 --> 00:07:47.740
So Agent stops using this test set, on the owner's behalf.

43
00:07:55.256 --> 00:08:02.312
The plan allowed one retry for a failure that a retry can fix.
The retry runs without the defective tests.

44
00:08:06.256 --> 00:08:09.880
The card shows the same limit, and no QA test set.

45
00:08:13.256 --> 00:08:15.128
Agent confirms it.

46
00:08:31.256 --> 00:08:38.552
The retry is verified in one round: build, unit tests,
acceptance tests, the UI check, and scope.

47
00:08:42.256 --> 00:08:44.224
It cost under three cents.

48
00:09:01.256 --> 00:09:08.912
Now the review. Agent reviews, so the review is marked Agent.
Agent reviews never count as owner evidence.

49
00:09:13.256 --> 00:09:19.112
The timer started when the page opened. The reviewer reads the
diff and the browser screenshots of the run.

50
00:09:28.256 --> 00:09:34.736
Both page requirements are met. The scores are saved as a draft:
five, four, four, five.

51
00:09:44.256 --> 00:09:49.656
The review mode is independent. The Judge advice stays hidden
until the scores are locked.

52
00:10:01.256 --> 00:10:08.168
The Judge is a second model. It reads a fixed evidence packet of
the run, and its advice never decides anything.

53
00:10:13.256 --> 00:10:19.232
The preview shows the exact attempt and a maximum cost of fifty
cents. Agent confirms it.

54
00:10:35.256 --> 00:10:41.328
The report is selected, and the four saved scores are locked.
Only now does the Judge advice appear.

55
00:10:48.256 --> 00:10:53.824
The Judge proposes five, five, four, five: close to the review.

56
00:10:58.256 --> 00:11:05.120
Each risk cites its evidence. The first one is real: a failed
request gives the user no feedback.

57
00:11:11.256 --> 00:11:16.968
The second one does not hold: its own evidence shows four of
four acceptance tests passed.

58
00:11:29.256 --> 00:11:34.536
The review is approved, after the Judge advice. Publication is a
separate step.

59
00:11:39.256 --> 00:11:46.600
Factory publishes a branch and a draft pull request with exactly
the verified files. Nothing merges by itself.

60
00:11:56.256 --> 00:11:59.832
Pull request number fifteen is open as a draft.

61
00:12:11.256 --> 00:12:18.552
Factory reads the CI result of the exact published commit. Here,
GitHub's build passed in forty seconds.

62
00:12:24.256 --> 00:12:31.168
The CI value page compares CI with Factory's own verification,
across all published pull requests.

63
00:12:49.256 --> 00:12:55.664
And the feature itself: TaskFlow from the draft pull request
branch, on this computer, not merged.

64
00:12:58.256 --> 00:13:04.040
Select Complete. The task leaves the Open tab, and the numbers
change without a page reload.

65
00:13:12.256 --> 00:13:14.512
It now appears on the Completed tab.

66
00:13:27.256 --> 00:13:36.400
Factory also measures itself. In a blind A/B experiment, the
owner scored two runtimes on the same task without knowing which
was which.

67
00:13:44.256 --> 00:13:50.880
A replay of a merged task passes only with its hidden tests and
the owner's review of the page requirements.

68
00:14:02.256 --> 00:14:08.544
And Cost and effort shows every value that was not measured as
Unknown, never as zero.

69
00:14:24.756 --> 00:14:32.148
What this demo does not prove: one small feature is not a
general result, and the review here is not a human review.

70
00:14:38.756 --> 00:14:46.076
All IDs and costs are on this card. The whole demo cost thirteen
cents of the approved two dollars and twenty cents.
